# {{name}} — notes for coding agents

This is a [Jongo](https://github.com/slimboi34/jongo) app: full-stack Python, one language for
the whole thing. The table, the server functions, the reactive UI and the pages all live in
`app.py`. There is no JavaScript project, no API schema and no build step.

## Commands

| | |
|---|---|
| `jongo dev` | run it at http://localhost:8000 with auto-reload and an error overlay |
| `jongo check --json` | compile every component, resolve every server function's type hints, plan migrations; problems come back as data and the exit code is 1 if there are any. **Run this after every change.** |
| `jongo context` | the live map of this app (models, server functions with signatures, pages, components, channels) plus the rules of the framework. **Run this before you start.** |
| `jongo routes --json` | the URL map |
| `pytest` | the tests (`from jongo.testing import TestClient` goes through the real HTTP stack) |

## The rules that matter most

- A `@component` runs on the server *and* in the browser (compiled to JavaScript), so it may only
  do what both can do: no database, no file system, no `import` inside a function. Load data in the
  page or a `@server` function and pass it as props. Anything else is a compile error `jongo check`
  will show you, with the file, the line and a hint.
- The browser calls the server with a plain `await add_thing(args)`; only `@server` functions are
  reachable and their arguments are validated against the type hints before the code runs. A parameter
  annotated with a model class receives the row for the id that was sent.
- Models are `db.Model` subclasses; the schema follows them automatically. No migration files.
- Keep it in one language. Do not add JavaScript, a frontend framework, or a dependency for something
  the framework already does (auth, sessions, CSRF, admin, real time, styles are built in).

Everything else, including the browser's few known differences from CPython, is in the output of
`jongo context`.
